WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.600
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:09.880
This is a piece of elastic cloth modeled from
more than a million tiny triangles and its

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.469
interaction with 7 million colored grains
of sand.

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:19.520
This is super challenging because of two things:
one, we have to compute the elastic deformations

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:25.530
when these millions of tiny elements collide,
and two, all this, while maintaining two-way

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:26.530
coupling.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:31.060
This means that the cloth has an effect on
the sand, but the effect of the sand is also

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:32.889
simulated on the cloth.

00:00:32.889 --> 00:00:37.199
In elastic deformations, there are potential
interactions between distant parts of the

00:00:37.199 --> 00:00:40.699
same material and self-collisions may also
occur.

00:00:40.699 --> 00:00:45.420
Previous state of the art techniques were
either lacking in these self-collision effects,

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:51.089
or the ones that were able to process that
also included the fracturing of the material.

00:00:51.089 --> 00:00:56.769
With this novel work, it is possible to simulate
both elastic deformations as you can see here,

00:00:56.769 --> 00:01:02.350
but, it also supports simulating plasticity
as you can see here with the cloth pieces

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.509
sliding off of each other.

00:01:04.509 --> 00:01:05.509
Beautiful!

00:01:05.509 --> 00:01:10.299
This new technique also supports simulating
a variety of different types of materials,

00:01:10.299 --> 00:01:16.689
knitted cloth ponchos, shag carpets, twisting
cloth, hair, tearing fiber and more.

00:01:16.689 --> 00:01:22.479
And it does all this with a typical execution
time between 10 to 90 seconds per frame.

00:01:22.479 --> 00:01:27.160
In these black screens, you see the timing
information and the number of particles and

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.649
triangles used in these simulations.

00:01:29.649 --> 00:01:34.020
And you will see that there are many scenes
where millions of triangles and particles

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.289
are processed in very little time.

00:01:36.289 --> 00:01:41.729
It is very rare that we can implement only
one technique that takes care of so many kinds

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:45.659
of interactions while still obtaining results
very quickly.

00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:46.659
This is insanity.

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:51.890
This paper is absolutely top tier bang for
the buck and I am really excited to see some

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:56.689
more elastoplastic simulations in all kinds
of digital media in the future.

00:01:56.689 --> 00:02:01.160
You know our motto, a couple more papers down
the line and having something like this in

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.189
real-time applications may become a reality.

00:02:04.189 --> 00:02:05.189
Really cool!

